1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,600
This is Doing and Feeling. Part 2, Feeling. Episode 7, The Hard Problem. In 1714, Gottfried Leibniz proposed a thought experiment that sits underneath every argument in this part of the series.

2
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:26,620
Suppose, he said, there were a machine whose structure made it think, feel, and perceive. Now imagine it enlarged, keeping all its proportions, until you could walk inside it the way you walk into a mill.

3
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:30,700
Inside, you would find only parts pushing against other parts.

4
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:34,000
Nowhere would you find anything that explains a perception.

5
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:39,680
Leibniz concluded that perception must belong to something simple, not to mechanism.

6
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:44,260
Swap the mill for a modern neural network, and you have the contemporary version.

7
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:51,080
Open up a large language model, and you find matrix multiplications and nonlinearities all the way down.

8
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:53,480
You can trace every number it computes,

9
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:57,400
and nowhere in the trace is there a place where things start to feel like something.

10
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:00,580
Open up a brain, and the story is the same,

11
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:04,280
with ion channels and neurotransmitters instead of matrices.

12
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:09,020
Notice the shape of the argument, because it's this whole episode in miniature.

13
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:15,240
It moves from I cannot see experience in the mechanism to experience is not in the mechanism.

14
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:19,080
The question for this episode is whether that move is valid

15
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:27,160
yes or no. Here's the route. A short history of how this became a respectable problem.

16
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:33,580
Then the three arguments that define the modern debate. Thomas Nagel's bat, Joseph Levine's

17
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:39,580
explanatory gap, and David Chalmers's hard problem, including the formal conceivability argument

18
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:45,660
and the full map of responses to it. Then Frank Jackson's Mary and the four serious replies to her.

19
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:50,600
Then the distinction between access and phenomenal consciousness, and an experiment

20
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:57,400
1960 that still divides the field. Then Wittgenstein. And finally, a first look at the

21
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:03,700
metaproblem, which the next episode puts to work. The problem has been noticed many times.

22
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:10,440
In the 1860s, Thomas Huxley wrote that the arrival of a state of consciousness from irritated nerve

23
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:15,500
tissue was as unaccountable as the genie appearing when Aladdin rubbed his lamp.

24
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:22,760
In 1872, the physiologist Emel du Biwa-Raymond gave a famous lecture on the limits of natural

25
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:28,420
science, and consciousness was on his list. His verdict on how matter produces sensation

26
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:34,800
was a single Latin word, ignorabimus, we shall never know. Then psychology spent much of the

27
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:40,320
20th century avoiding the subject. Behaviorism said that science should study behavior,

28
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:46,480
not interstates. In 1949, the philosopher Gilbert Ryle mocked the picture of a ghost

29
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:50,740
in the machine as a category mistake, like a visitor who is shown the colleges,

30
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:55,480
the libraries, and the laboratories, and then asks where the university is.

31
00:02:55,640 --> 00:03:01,060
The cognitive revolution brought back internal processing, but as information processing.

32
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:07,100
Experience itself stayed off the agenda. The modern revival came in the 1990s.

33
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:23,680
In 1994, Francis Crick, co-discoverer of the structure of DNA, published The Astonishing Hypothesis, arguing that your joys and sorrows, your memories, your sense of self and free will, come down to the activity of enormous numbers of neurons.

34
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:33,020
That same year, at a conference in Tucson, a young Australian philosopher named David Chalmers gave a talk that named the problem in a way that stuck.

35
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:38,600
Within a few years, there were dedicated journals, conferences, and a research program.

36
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:41,940
But the argument everyone reaches for first is older.

37
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:50,400
Thomas Nagel's 1974 essay, What Is It Like to Be a Bat?, is 16 pages long and makes four moves.

38
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:52,840
The fourth is The One People Forget.

39
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:59,220
First, a definition. A creature is conscious, Nagel says, when there is something it is like

40
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:04,820
to be that creature, for the creature itself. That phrase, something it is like, became the

41
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:10,000
standard way of picking out what philosophers call phenomenal consciousness. Second, that

42
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,300
subjective character is tied to a point of view. Bats perceive the world by echolocation,

43
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:21,740
they emit high-pitched calls and build a picture from the echoes. We can study sonar exhaustively,

44
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:29,620
that way is fully accessible only from a bat's kind of point of view. Imagining yourself hanging

45
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:34,980
upside down with webbed arms does not help. That tells you what it would be like for you to behave

46
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:41,420
like a bat, not what it is like for a bat to be a bat. Third, and this is the key step, scientific

47
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:46,740
reduction normally works by moving away from particular points of view toward more objective

48
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:52,720
description. We understood lightning by setting aside how it looks to human eyes and describing

49
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:58,900
discharge. But if the thing you're trying to explain is the point of view itself, then moving

50
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:04,440
away from points of view moves you away from the very thing you wanted to explain. Fourth, the

51
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:10,920
conclusion. Nagel did not say physicalism is false. He said that we currently have no idea how it could

52
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:17,080
be true. His analogy was the claim that matter is energy, put forward by a philosopher in ancient

53
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:23,300
Greece. It might have been true, but no one then had the concepts to see how. Nagel even proposed

54
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:29,660
program he called objective phenomenology, a way of describing the structure of experiences

55
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:35,100
that does not require having them. That program is still mostly a promissory note,

56
00:05:35,240 --> 00:05:41,080
but it's worth remembering that the most famous anti-reductionist essay of the 20th century ends

57
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:45,160
by asking for better science, not by declaring it impossible.

58
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:50,660
Joseph Levine sharpened the point in 1983 with a phrase that stuck,

59
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:56,840
the explanatory gap. Compare two identity claims. Heat is the motion of molecules,

60
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:03,180
and pain is the firing of sea fibers, which, by the way, is a philosopher's toy example

61
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:08,160
rather than real neuroscience. In the heat case, once you understand molecular motion,

62
00:06:08,460 --> 00:06:13,760
nothing is left over. Molecular motion does everything heat does. It raises thermometers,

63
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:19,820
melts ice, burns your hand. There's no residual question. In the pain case, even if the identity

64
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:25,400
could still ask, why does that firing feel like this, rather than like something else or like

65
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:31,300
nothing at all? The identity might hold, but it does not explain. Levine's point was epistemic,

66
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:37,200
not metaphysical. He was not claiming that pain is non-physical. He was claiming that our

67
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:43,000
explanations leave a gap that our explanations of heat do not. Whether that gap reflects something

68
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,480
about the world or only about the limits of our concepts is what the rest of the debate is about.

69
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:52,700
Heat had a gap, too, until statistical mechanics closed it.

70
00:06:52,980 --> 00:06:55,100
Why think this one is different in kind?

71
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:59,440
That's the hope of a position we'll meet shortly, Type C physicalism.

72
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,920
The worry is about how the heat gap closed.

73
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:06,240
Statistical mechanics showed that molecular motion does everything heat does.

74
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:10,600
It explained heat's functions, and for heat there was nothing more to explain.

75
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:14,060
If experience is something over and above its functions,

76
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:17,820
a story about functions cannot close the gap the same way.

77
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:21,900
Whether it is something more is exactly what Chalmers' argument is about.

78
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:24,680
Which brings us to Chalmers.

79
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:29,640
In his 1995 paper, Facing Up to the Problem of Consciousness,

80
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:32,860
and his 1996 book, The Conscious Mind,

81
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:36,900
he separated the easy problems of consciousness from the hard problem.

82
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:39,840
The easy problems are about explaining functions,

83
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:43,320
how we discriminate stimuli, integrate information,

84
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:46,260
report our mental states, focus attention,

85
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:50,000
deliberately control behavior, and how waking differs from sleep.

86
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:52,540
They're easy only in a special sense.

87
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:56,720
They may take centuries to solve, but we know what kind of thing an answer is,

88
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:59,040
a mechanism that performs the function.

89
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:04,300
The hard problem is why the performance of those functions is accompanied by experience.

90
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:06,560
Chalmers' claim is structural.

91
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:12,420
Once you've explained every function, it still seems a further question why any of it is felt.

92
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:15,180
Function does not appear to entail feeling.

93
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:32,680
And if that were the whole argument, you'd be right to.

94
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:36,660
So Chalmers made it explicit, and it's worth going through slowly.

95
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,260
This is the conceivability argument.

96
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:42,440
Premise 1. A zombie world is conceivable.

97
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:49,080
Picture a world physically identical to ours, down to the last particle, in which nobody experiences anything.

98
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:56,040
Your zombie twin talks about the redness of sunsets and the pain of stubbed toes, but there's nothing it's like to be it.

99
00:08:56,260 --> 00:09:02,760
Not conceivable in a lazy sense, but ideally conceivable, surviving any amount of careful reflection.

100
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:08,300
Premise 2. If something is ideally conceivable, then it's metaphysically possible.

101
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:09,600
Premise 3.

102
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:11,720
If a zombie world is possible,

103
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:15,540
then the physical facts do not necessitate the facts about experience,

104
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:17,360
and physicalism is false.

105
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:18,520
Conclusion.

106
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:20,020
Physicalism is false.

107
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:21,820
The argument is valid,

108
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:24,440
so every response has to reject a premise.

109
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:29,180
And Chalmers himself drew the map of responses in a 2003 paper

110
00:09:29,180 --> 00:09:31,220
that sorts positions by letter.

111
00:09:31,540 --> 00:09:35,000
It's the most useful map in the field, so here it is.

112
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:37,580
Type of physicalists deny premise 1.

113
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:40,460
Daniel Dennett and the illusionists live here.

114
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:43,280
Zombies only seem conceivable, they say,

115
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:46,660
because we have not really imagined everything we're stipulating.

116
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:49,300
Dennett's comparison is vitalism.

117
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:53,320
Picture someone who, after every function of life has been explained,

118
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:56,880
still asks why all that is accompanied by life itself.

119
00:09:57,120 --> 00:10:01,520
The question sounds deep until you realize that being alive just is metabolism,

120
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:04,200
growth, reproduction, and the rest.

121
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:07,480
Once those are explained, nothing is left over.

122
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:13,800
this view, experience is the same. Explain every function and there's no further ingredient,

123
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:19,160
and zombies seem conceivable only because we never really imagine all the functions at once.

124
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:25,980
Type B physicalists accept premise 1 but deny premise 2. Brian Lohr, David Papineau,

125
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:32,100
and Ned Block are among them. Their model is water. Before modern chemistry, it seemed perfectly

126
00:10:32,100 --> 00:10:37,760
conceivable that water was something other than H2O. But Saul Kripke showed that identity statements

127
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:55,760
That sounds like the obvious answer.

128
00:10:56,500 --> 00:11:00,200
Conceivability is a fact about our concepts, not about the world.

129
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,680
So what stops type B physicalism from simply winning?

130
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:07,200
Partly Kripke himself, which is a lovely twist.

131
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:14,320
and necessity, he argued that pain is not like water. With water, we can explain away the feeling

132
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:20,460
of contingency. When we seem to imagine water that is not H2O, what we're really imagining is

133
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:26,800
some watery-looking stuff that is not H2O, fool's water, so to speak. With pain, the trick fails.

134
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:32,820
Anything that feels like pain simply is pain. There's no gap between how pain appears and what

135
00:11:32,820 --> 00:11:38,620
it is, so you cannot say you merely imagined something that seemed like pain. Kripke used

136
00:11:38,620 --> 00:11:43,060
this against identity theories, and type B physicalists have been working around it ever

137
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:50,100
since. Chalmers added a formal tool, two-dimensional semantics, to say exactly when conceivability does

138
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:56,200
entail possibility. Very roughly, it separates what a word picks out in the actual world from

139
00:11:56,200 --> 00:12:01,900
the way it picks things out. Type B physicalists reject the tool, and the dispute continues in

140
00:12:03,300 --> 00:12:09,340
Type C physicalism says the gap is real now, but will close once we have concepts we do not yet

141
00:12:09,340 --> 00:12:14,300
possess. Chalmers argues that under pressure it collapses into one of the other letters,

142
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:21,340
physicalist or not. Then come the non-physicalist letters. Type D is interactionist dualism.

143
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:28,280
Experience is non-physical and pushes matter around. Type E is epiphenomenalism. Experience

144
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:34,900
is non-physical and does nothing at all. And typef is Russellian monism, the family that includes

145
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:40,620
many forms of panpsychism, on which experience is the hidden intrinsic nature of what physics

146
00:12:40,620 --> 00:12:47,080
describes only from the outside. Keep these six letters in mind. Typef gets the whole of the next

147
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:54,280
episode, and the rest are the skeleton of episode 9. Frank Jackson's knowledge argument from 1982

148
00:12:54,280 --> 00:12:59,860
approaches the same territory from another direction. Mary is a brilliant scientist who

149
00:12:59,860 --> 00:13:05,000
knows every physical fact about color vision, the wavelengths, the retinal cones, every neural

150
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:10,180
pathway. But she has lived her whole life in a black and white room. One day she walks out and

151
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:15,680
sees a ripe tomato. Does she learn something new? If she does, then knowing all the physical facts

152
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:20,560
was not knowing all the facts. There are four main replies, and they're worth having straight.

153
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:24,780
The Ability Hypothesis from David Lewis and Lawrence Nemiro

154
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:27,660
Mary gains abilities rather than facts,

155
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:31,540
the ability to imagine read, to recognize it, to remember it.

156
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:33,440
Know how, not know that.

157
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:37,140
The objection is that it really does seem she learned something statable,

158
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:39,100
that seeing read is like this.

159
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:41,240
The Acquaintance Hypothesis

160
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:45,900
Mary becomes directly acquainted with a property she already knew all about,

161
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:48,100
a new relation to an old property.

162
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:50,280
Then the most influential reply,

163
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:56,700
the phenomenal concept strategy. Mary acquires a new concept, a recognitional concept that points

164
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:01,920
at the experience from the inside, something like this kind of state. That new concept picks

165
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:06,760
out a physical property she already knew under a different, scientific concept, the way the

166
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:12,700
morning star and the evening star turned out to be the same planet. Same fact, new way of thinking

167
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:19,040
about it. The strategy has a second attraction. If phenomenal concepts and physical concepts are

168
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:25,860
heads, you'd expect to be able to pull them apart in imagination, even if the things they

169
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:31,660
refer to cannot come apart. So the strategy explains why zombies seem conceivable without

170
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:36,360
adding anything to the world. It explains the intuition rather than honoring it.

171
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:42,440
That's the move that feels right to me as an engineer. When a model is confidently wrong,

172
00:14:42,700 --> 00:14:46,400
you explain the confidence. You do not treat it as evidence.

173
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:49,220
It's attractive for exactly that reason.

174
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:55,680
Chalmers replied in 2007 with what's sometimes called his master argument, which is a dilemma.

175
00:14:56,100 --> 00:15:00,420
Ask whether your zombie twin has the same phenomenal concepts you have.

176
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:05,640
If it does, then those concepts cannot explain your special epistemic situation,

177
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:08,600
since the zombie has them without any experience.

178
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:14,180
If it does not, then phenomenal concepts cannot be explained in physical terms after all.

179
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:17,340
Either way, he argues, the strategy fails.

180
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:19,480
Its defenders have replies,

181
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:22,820
and this is one of the liveliest disputes in the field.

182
00:15:23,140 --> 00:15:24,940
The fourth reply is Dennett's.

183
00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:28,080
He argued that a Mary who truly knew everything physical

184
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:29,520
would not be surprised at all.

185
00:15:29,740 --> 00:15:31,900
Show her a blue banana as a trick,

186
00:15:31,940 --> 00:15:33,180
and she'd say at once,

187
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:35,300
that is not what yellow looks like.

188
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:36,620
And one more twist.

189
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:39,560
Jackson himself later abandoned the argument

190
00:15:39,560 --> 00:15:41,080
and became a physicalist,

191
00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:44,420
explaining experience in terms of mental representation.

192
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:47,640
The argument outlived its author's belief in it.

193
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:52,900
Next, a distinction that quietly causes half the confusion in this area.

194
00:15:53,260 --> 00:15:57,460
In 1995, Ned Block distinguished phenomenal consciousness,

195
00:15:57,800 --> 00:16:00,300
the what it's like, from access consciousness,

196
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:05,820
information being available for reasoning, for report, and for controlling behavior.

197
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:09,120
Block's point was that the two are conceptually distinct,

198
00:16:11,140 --> 00:16:17,120
slide between them. His example was blindsight, which we will meet properly in episode 10.

199
00:16:17,380 --> 00:16:22,640
People with damage to visual cortex who deny seeing anything in part of their visual field

200
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:27,620
yet can guess about objects they're far better than chance. It's tempting to reason that,

201
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:33,880
because these patients lack visual experience and also lack certain abilities, experience must be

202
00:16:33,880 --> 00:16:39,880
what supplies those abilities. Block called this a fallacy. The patients also lack access.

203
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:46,340
which missing ingredient explains the missing abilities. Block then went further and argued

204
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:52,160
that phenomenal consciousness overflows access. His evidence starts with an experiment by George

205
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:59,360
Sperling from 1960. Flash a grid of 12 letters, three rows of four, for a fraction of a second.

206
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:05,500
Ask people to report everything, and they manage about four. But if a tone just after the flash

207
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:10,520
tells them which row to report, they can get most of that row, whichever row it is.

208
00:17:11,080 --> 00:17:16,420
So for a moment, most of the grid was available, even though only a fraction can ever be reported.

209
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:21,800
Bloch's interpretation is that you consciously see the whole grid, specific letters, and all,

210
00:17:21,980 --> 00:17:23,820
and that what's limited is access.

211
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:26,460
Experience is richer than report.

212
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:32,120
He made the case in 2007 in a Target article in Behavioral and Brain Sciences,

213
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:38,680
the format we met in Episode 5, and its commentaries are a fine place to watch a field argue with itself.

214
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:47,260
On the other side, Michael Cohen and Daniel Dennett argued in 2011 that experiences inaccessible in principle could never be tested.

215
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:56,940
Others, such as the philosopher Ian Phillips, suggested that people may experience something more generic, letter-like shapes rather than specific letters.

216
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:01,460
Underneath, this lies what's often called the refrigerator light problem.

217
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:05,280
Whenever you check whether you're conscious of something, you access it.

218
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:09,580
So you can never catch experience in the act of existing without access,

219
00:18:09,860 --> 00:18:13,400
just as you can never open the fridge door and see the light off.

220
00:18:13,700 --> 00:18:17,380
So this is a measurement problem wearing a metaphysics costume.

221
00:18:17,680 --> 00:18:20,640
Every probe you can build is an access probe.

222
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:22,340
Largely, yes.

223
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:26,020
And it becomes a major empirical fight in episode 11

224
00:18:26,020 --> 00:18:30,500
because two leading scientific theories line up on opposite sides of it.

225
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:33,940
A few more distinctions will keep the rest of the series clear.

226
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:40,280
separate creature consciousness being awake rather than in a coma from state consciousness,

227
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:46,620
a particular mental state being conscious rather than unconscious. Scientists separate the level

228
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:52,540
of consciousness, its contents, and the conscious self. And some researchers, including Tim Bain,

229
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:59,760
argue that even talking about levels is misleading. Dreaming, psychedelic states, sedation, and minimal

230
00:18:59,760 --> 00:19:05,160
consciousness after brain injury differ along several dimensions at once, rather than sitting

231
00:19:05,280 --> 00:19:10,980
at different points on one dial. Ludwig Wittgenstein enters the story from an angle.

232
00:19:11,260 --> 00:19:16,840
In the Philosophical Investigations, he imagines that everyone has a box containing something they

233
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:22,800
call a beetle. No one can look into anyone else's box, and everyone knows what a beetle is only from

234
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:28,060
their own case. His point is that in such a language game, the thing in the box drops out

235
00:19:28,060 --> 00:19:34,180
of the picture entirely. The word beetle gets its meaning from its public use, whatever is or is not

236
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:38,540
That image belongs to his larger private language argument,

237
00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:44,080
the claim that words for sensations cannot get their meaning from private inner objects

238
00:19:44,080 --> 00:19:46,260
that only the speaker can inspect.

239
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:50,000
And in a passage that every student of this problem should read,

240
00:19:50,220 --> 00:19:55,280
he describes the feeling of an unbridgeable gulf between consciousness and brain processes.

241
00:19:55,580 --> 00:19:58,420
It does not arise in ordinary life, he says.

242
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:01,940
It arises when you turn your attention on your own consciousness,

243
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:07,700
in a philosophical mood, and ask in astonishment how this could possibly come from a process in

244
00:20:07,700 --> 00:20:13,800
the brain. Notice the word this and the gesture inward that goes with it. Wittgenstein treated

245
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:19,760
that astonishment as a symptom to be examined, not a discovery to be honored. Wittgenstein's

246
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:25,600
influence runs through behaviorism, through Dennett, and into the contemporary view called

247
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:31,000
illusionism. He did not try to solve the hard problem. He tried to show that it might be a

248
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:38,420
That astonishment, the inward gesture, and the sense of a gulf is itself something people do.

249
00:20:38,680 --> 00:20:42,120
They say it, write it down, and argue about it on podcasts.

250
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:46,900
In 2018, Chalmers turned that observation into a research program.

251
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:50,580
Alongside the hard problem, he proposed the meta-problem.

252
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,500
Explain why we think there is a hard problem.

253
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:57,900
Why do brains produce reports like I cannot explain my own experience?

254
00:20:58,440 --> 00:21:00,160
Because those reports are behavior,

255
00:21:00,660 --> 00:21:03,920
explaining them is, in principle, an easy problem,

256
00:21:04,380 --> 00:21:07,240
one that realists and their opponents can work on together

257
00:21:07,240 --> 00:21:09,360
while expecting different kinds of answer.

258
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:13,340
I think it's the most productive development in this debate in decades,

259
00:21:13,340 --> 00:21:15,560
and the next episode builds on it.

260
00:21:16,500 --> 00:21:19,680
Let me step back and name the strategies on the table.

261
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:22,060
The first is to dissolve the problem,

262
00:21:22,340 --> 00:21:25,220
show that it rests on a confusion or an illusion.

263
00:21:25,900 --> 00:21:30,640
That's Wittgenstein's spirit and the illusionist program of Dennett and Keith Frankish.

264
00:21:31,020 --> 00:21:33,060
The second is to reduce the problem.

265
00:21:33,440 --> 00:21:38,480
Accept that experience is real and physical and explain why it seems otherwise.

266
00:21:38,980 --> 00:21:42,760
That's type B physicalism, the phenomenal concept strategy,

267
00:21:42,980 --> 00:21:49,020
and the representationalist theories that identify experience with a certain kind of mental representation.

268
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:52,220
The third is to expand our picture of reality.

269
00:21:52,760 --> 00:21:56,440
Accept that physics as we know it leaves something out and add to it.

270
00:21:56,940 --> 00:21:58,860
Dualism adds a new kind of property.

271
00:21:59,540 --> 00:22:04,900
Panpsychism and Russellian monism say experience is the intrinsic nature of matter.

272
00:22:05,520 --> 00:22:08,180
Idealism says experience is all there is.

273
00:22:08,520 --> 00:22:11,680
And where do professional philosophers actually land?

274
00:22:11,820 --> 00:22:13,600
Is there a majority view?

275
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:15,240
There's survey data.

276
00:22:15,480 --> 00:22:19,600
In the 2020 Philpapers Survey of Professional Philosophers,

277
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:26,100
about 62% said there is a hard problem of consciousness, and about 30% said there is not.

278
00:22:26,300 --> 00:22:32,460
At the same time, about 52% accepted or leaned toward physicalism about the mind,

279
00:22:32,800 --> 00:22:39,260
against 32% for non-physicalism. On consciousness specifically, functionalism was the most popular

280
00:22:39,260 --> 00:22:44,440
single view at about a third, then dualism at about a fifth, with identity theory,

281
00:22:44,440 --> 00:22:47,200
panpsychism, and eliminativism trailing.

282
00:22:47,620 --> 00:22:50,540
Put that together and you get the field's real position.

283
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:53,560
Most philosophers think the problem is genuine

284
00:22:53,560 --> 00:22:57,040
and a narrow majority still expect a physical answer.

285
00:22:57,520 --> 00:22:59,440
Few think it has been delivered yet.

286
00:23:00,380 --> 00:23:03,860
Eliminativism is the one that says consciousness does not exist.

287
00:23:04,140 --> 00:23:07,980
With under 5%, why give it more airtime than the others?

288
00:23:08,340 --> 00:23:10,680
Because the small number hides two things.

289
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:15,480
First, the label eliminativism sounds like denying that you feel pain,

290
00:23:15,680 --> 00:23:18,080
which no serious defender of the view does.

291
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:21,860
What they deny is that experience has the special private,

292
00:23:22,160 --> 00:23:24,780
ineffable qualities philosophers call qualia.

293
00:23:25,300 --> 00:23:29,040
Critics reply that this is denying consciousness by another name.

294
00:23:29,280 --> 00:23:32,060
Most defenders prefer the label illusionism.

295
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:35,940
Second, it's the position that most changes the question we care about.

296
00:23:36,240 --> 00:23:37,920
If illusionism is right,

297
00:23:37,920 --> 00:23:41,120
asking whether a machine is conscious stops being a mystery

298
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:45,800
and becomes a question you could, in principle, answer by looking inside it.

299
00:23:46,020 --> 00:23:50,860
So next episode, we give the most radical answer the strongest hearing we can

300
00:23:50,860 --> 00:23:53,360
and then put it through its hardest objections.

